<!--
    kuna: a 64-bit value a function builds in its one return register from two
    32-bit halves is returned whole, and every argument that feeds it stays a
    parameter.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 call and prototype model, output-prototype (the
    return-pair repair in kuna_returnuncomputed).  A strict fix: no option.

    Source (x86-64 SysV, bytes verbatim below; the fixture binaries and the
    compiled round trip are kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/piecehi_*):

        typedef unsigned long u64;
        u64 join_lo_hi(unsigned lo, unsigned hi) { return ((u64)hi << 32) | lo; }
        u64 join_third(unsigned a, unsigned b, unsigned c) { return ((u64)c << 32) | b; }
        u64 join_sixth(unsigned a, unsigned b, unsigned c, unsigned d, unsigned e,
                       unsigned f) { return ((u64)f << 32) | e; }
        long join_signed(int lo, int hi) { return ((long)hi << 32) | (unsigned)lo; }
        u64 join_hi_sum(unsigned a, unsigned b) { return ((u64)(a + 1) << 32) | b; }
        u64 join_lo_sum(unsigned a, unsigned b) { return ((u64)b << 32) | (a + 1); }
        u64 join_hi_lo(unsigned hi, unsigned lo) { return ((u64)hi << 32) | lo; }

    gcc -O0 for all of them at 0x1189 and 0x12cd; gcc -O2 join_hi_sum at 0x2410:
    endbr64 ; lea 0x1(%rdi),%eax ; mov %esi,%esi ; shl $0x20,%rax ; or %rsi,%rax ; ret

    The bug: the return-pair repair, which drops a half of a two-register
    return the function never wrote, read these one-register values as such a
    pair and judged each half by whether its source sat at the half's OWN
    address.  An argument register folded straight into the value (ESI in
    RAX's high half) always does, so it counted as the caller's leftover: the
    half was dropped, the return narrowed to four bytes, and the argument lost
    its only reader.  join_lo_hi printed `uint4 join_lo_hi(uint4 a0)` returning
    a0; join_hi_sum at -O2 printed `return a0 + 1;`, the high half alone.
    join_hi_lo, whose high half is the first argument, was already right.

    The fix: a half is judged against the bytes of the return register it
    occupies, and the high half of one register is never returned by itself.
-->
